When creating a manual image, you will use a technician machine to create a bootable environment, build your reference system in a Virtual Machine (VM), and then capture it.

The Manual ISO/WIM Creation Workflow

[Create Clean OS VM] ➔ [Enter Audit Mode] ➔ [Mass Install Software] ➔ [Sysprep & Generalize] ➔ [Capture to WIM]
1. Start with a Clean Base: Create a new Virtual Machine (Hyper-V, VMware, or VirtualBox) and boot it using a standard, untouched Windows ISO.
2. Enter Audit Mode Immediately: When the Windows installation finishes and reaches the initial setup screen (OOBE/Region Selection), do not create a user account. Instead, press Ctrl + Shift + F3. The VM will reboot into the built-in Administrator account.
3. Mass Install Software: Disconnect the internet to prevent Windows Update from pulling background AppX updates. Use Chocolatey or your silent MSI scripts to install all standard desktop applications (Chrome, VLC, Office, etc.).
4. Run Sysprep: Open the Sysprep GUI (located at C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\sysprep.exe), choose Generalize, select OOBE, and set the shutdown option to Shutdown.
5. Capture the WIM: Boot the VM into a Windows PE (WinPE) environment. Use the DISM tool to capture the C:\ drive into a custom.wim or install.wim file:cmd
dism /Capture-Image /ImageFile:D:\install.wim /CaptureDir:C:\ /Name:"Windows11GoldenImage"
Use code with caution.
6. Inject into the ISO: Use a tool like AnyBurn or OSCDIMG to open your original Windows ISO, swap out the default install.wim in the sources folder with your newly captured install.wim, and save it as a new bootable ISO.

Pro-Tips for Manual WIM Building

• Use Snapshots: Take a VM snapshot right after entering Audit Mode but before installing software. If Sysprep fails later, you can roll back instantly without re-installing Windows.
• Keep Internet Disconnected: Keep the network adapter disconnected in the VM during your software installations. This prevents Microsoft Store apps from auto-updating in the background, which is the primary cause of manual image breakage.
